      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/28/world/middleeast/syria-sednaya-prison-survivor.html</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 28 Nov 2026 16:22:55 +0000</pubDate>
      <enclosure url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/28/multimedia/28syria-prisoner-01-bflz/28syria-prisoner-01-bflz-mediumSquareAt3X.jpg" length="0" type="image/jpeg" />
      <media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/28/multimedia/28syria-prisoner-01-bflz/28syria-prisoner-01-bflz-mediumSquareAt3X.jpg" medium="image" />
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/28/multimedia/28syria-prisoner-01-bflz/28syria-prisoner-01-bflz-mediumSquareAt3X.jpg" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;margin-bottom:10px;" alt="Freed From a Notorious Syrian Prison, but Still Trapped by Its Terror" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Ahmed al-Shalh expected to perish in Sednaya prison. Abruptly released after a decade, he reveled in marriage and fatherhood but struggled to shake his ghosts.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
